\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Discussion}
\title{Potentials on points, differences on edges}
\date{2026-11-03}


\p{The classical field we know best is the electromagnetic field. In
electrostatics the potential #{\varphi} assigns a number to each point
of space, the field #{E=-\nabla\varphi} is what a test charge feels,
and #{\varphi} is fixed only up to an added constant, which #{E} does
not see. In the language of differential forms, #{\varphi} is a
#{0}-form and #{E=-d\varphi} is a #{1}-form: #{d} takes a function on
points to something evaluated along directions, and it kills constants.
The vector potential #{A} is a #{1}-form too, the field strength is
#{F=dA}, and #{d\circ d=0} says that a gradient has no curl. Geometric
calculus puts the same thing through the vector derivative:
#{\nabla\varphi} is a vector field, and #{a\cdot\nabla\varphi} is the
rate of change of #{\varphi} along a direction #{a}, so comparing
nearby values is again tied to a direction.}

\p{On a lattice only finitely many points remain, and a direction
becomes a step from one site to a neighbour. What is left of the
gradient when there is no limit to take, only the values at the two
ends of each step? And if comparing values is nothing but subtraction,
what kinds of values may a field take?}
